## Semantic Color Rules

Status colors communicate machine or workflow state. They are never decoration.

| Color | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Teal | #38B2AC | OK, connected, done, pass; positive commit |
| Amber | #FFD740 | Running, pending, unsaved, selected mode, needs attention; Help |
| Red | #FF5252 | Error, fail, priority, destructive; leave / abort |
| Signal yellow | #FFE600 | The next expected action; transient highlight |

Permitted forms:
1. Text color -- alert headings, status readout text, critical identifiers, key-value values, exit/skip button labels.
2. Fill of a small indicator -- LED, status pill, step pill, test-result box, priority badge, mode badge.
3. Fill of a semantic action button -- Signal (yellow), Confirm (teal), Danger (red), Attention (amber).
4. Accent bar -- 8px left border of a banner; 4-5px left border of a blockquote or category label.
5. Progress fill and the moving-stripe "working" texture.
6. Border color of a small item to flag it (error FAQ item, priority card, search hit).
7. Whole-bar state fill, only for: environment banner, unsaved-settings container, status header block of a hero screen.
8. Pale tint behind a message (--tint-*), always with black text and a black border.

Never:
- Color page backgrounds, layout cards, sidebars, panel headers or structural borders.
- Use color without redundancy: every colored state also has a glyph, a label, or a position that says the same thing.
- Put two different status colors on the same element (a tint plus the matching status accent counts as one).
- Use more than one Signal/Confirm/Danger button in the same dialog, or more than one amber Attention button per screen.
- Introduce a new hue. If something is not OK / pending / error / next-action, it is black, white or gray.

Text on status fills: white on teal and red, black on amber and signal yellow. Use weight 800 and at least 11px on any colored fill.

Categorical data colors: when the domain needs to distinguish product lines or variants (e.g. two product families, three frame colors), define them as a small, separate, clearly named palette per project and confine them to overview panels, filter swatches and list badges -- 3px colored border on a pale tint for a category panel, solid fill for a variant swatch. They never leave data visualisation and never replace status colors.

---

## Effects and Animation

Motion Philosophy: Mechanical and Instant. The interface mimics physical hardware. The button press is the primary animation; state changes are instant.

Allowed motion (complete list):
```
Button / key / tile state change      transition: all 0.1s ease-out
List item hover                        transition: background 0.1s ease
LED and readout state change           transition: all 0.1s ease
Log panel collapse / expand            transition: height 0.2s ease; arrow rotates 0.2s ease; log fades 0.1s
Unsaved-settings highlight             transition: background-color 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease
Toast appear / disappear               transition: opacity 0.2s ease
Progress fill width                    0.1s ease inline, 0.45s ease-out on the hero bar
Spinner                                spin 3s linear infinite
"Working" stripes                      progress-stripe 1.2s linear infinite / moveStripes 1.1s linear infinite
Running pulse (pills, simple LEDs)     pulse 1s infinite alternate (opacity 0.4 to 1)
Blink dot                              blinkDot 1.1s steps(2, start) infinite
Attention pulse on a container         border-color to --status-err and back, 1.2s ease-in-out, once
```

Not allowed:
- Fade or slide transitions for dialogs, views or tabs -- they appear and disappear instantly
- Scale-in / pop animations, bouncy or spring easing
- Parallax, floating, shimmer or skeleton effects
- Any blur except the scrim backdrop
- Animated layout changes other than the log panel

---

## Iconography and Copy

Icons: emoji, for zero-dependency cross-platform rendering in a kiosk. Used as a prefix to a text label in a separate `span` with `gap: 6px`. The text label must be self-sufficient; an emoji never stands alone, except the standard glyphs listed below. Icon size follows the surrounding font size.

Typical prefixes: open book U+1F4D6 (help), antenna bars U+1F4F6 (Wi-Fi), sun U+2600 (brightness), electric plug U+1F50C (devices), warning sign U+26A0 (alert headings), package U+1F4E6, printer U+1F5A8, clipboard U+1F4CB, counterclockwise arrows U+1F504 (refresh / retry), test tube U+1F9EA (test mode), lock U+1F512 / open lock U+1F513, hourglass U+23F3 (waiting).

Standard glyphs (monochrome, set in JetBrains Mono): check mark U+2713, ballot X U+2717, horizontal ellipsis U+2026, multiplication X U+2715 (close), heavy multiplication X U+2716 (close fullscreen), black up-pointing triangle U+25B2 (collapse), black circle U+25CF / white circle U+25CB (phase state), heavy arrow U+2794 (before -> after), leftwards arrow U+2190 (backspace).

Copy rules:
- Buttons are short imperative verbs or nouns in uppercase: START DEPLOY, RESET SCAN, EXIT, RETRY, OK.
- Affirmative buttons in critical dialogs restate the consequence: "YES, DELETE", "OK -- PACKED", "UNDERSTOOD AND CONTINUE".
- Operator-facing sentences are in the operators` language, short and direct, without apology or filler; established technical terms stay as they are (Deploy, Reboot, Hotspot, Firmware).
- Waiting states name what is happening: "PLEASE WAIT..." + what is being done.
- Technical values are shown verbatim in JetBrains Mono, never paraphrased.
- Empty and loading states are a single centered muted bold line ("Loading orders...", "No devices"); errors in the same position use --status-err.
- System-level texts use square brackets and wide tracking.

---

## Implementation Conventions

- Stack in the reference: Electron renderer pages, plain HTML + one `styles.css` per tool + vanilla JS, no build step, no UI framework, no CSS framework. Each tool repeats the same token block and foundation rules at the top of its stylesheet. In a new project, share ONE foundation stylesheet instead.
- Markup: all interactive elements are `button type="button"`, `input` or `select` -- never a `div` with a click handler (tiles and list items are the tolerated exceptions and must then get `cursor: pointer` and press feedback). Labels wrap or reference their input.
- Dialogs are static markup at the end of `body`, hidden with `display: none` and shown by setting `display: flex` on the overlay. No dialog library, no portal.
- State is expressed with classes: `state-0..3` on LEDs, `active` on tabs/toggles/filters, `expanded` on the log panel, `step-running / step-ok / step-fail` on pills, `banner-ok / banner-warn / banner-err`, `settings-dirty`, `is-prio`, `fullscreen`.
- Base class + modifier: `.btn-neu` + `.btn-sys` / `.btn-small` / `.btn-neu-primary`; `.neu-card` + role class (`.control-panel`, `.log-panel`, `.global-panel`, `.modal-content`).
- Hover and active rules carry `!important` so that every color variant inverts identically. Do not fight this with more specific selectors.
- Do not style with inline `style` attributes in new code. The reference contains many inline styles for one-off dialogs; their values are captured in this file as components -- implement them as classes.
- Dynamically generated markup (lists, chips, test results) uses the same classes as static markup. Escape all external text before inserting it.
- Never use native `alert()` / `confirm()`; use the alert and confirm dialogs.
- Scrolling regions get `overflow-y: auto` and a parent with `min-height: 0`.

---

## Accessibility

Contrast: #000000 on #FFFFFF = 21:1. Muted text #525252 on white = 7.8:1. All structural text meets WCAG AAA. Text on amber and signal yellow is always black. White text on teal or red is restricted to bold uppercase labels of 11px and up plus a redundant glyph or wording.

Touch targets:
- Primary flow: 48-80px tall (launcher 200 x 70px, hero 80px, standard 54px, dialog 48-60px)
- Utility and navigation: 40-44px tall
- Dense inline secondary controls: 32-36px tall, only inside dense panels and never the only way to reach a function
- Keyboard keys: flex width x 42px
- Checkbox: 28px box, whole label row tappable
- Minimum gap between adjacent targets: 6px (keys), 12px (buttons)

Focus: `button:focus-visible, select:focus-visible, input:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #000; outline-offset: 2px; }`. Inputs additionally thicken their border and switch to #F9F9F9 on focus. No colored focus rings.

Redundancy: every status is conveyed by color + glyph or color + text.

Hover: all hover effects are inside `@media (hover: hover)` so nothing sticks on touchscreens.

Semantic HTML: real buttons and inputs, labels bound to inputs, heading hierarchy h2 -> h3 -> h4 inside panels and dialogs.

Sleep state: black overlay with a visible bracketed wake hint; the entire overlay is the wake target.

---

## Derivation Rules (for components not listed here)

1. Is it pressable? Then: 2px black border, hard shadow from the scale (4px primary, 2px utility), rest shift of half the shadow up-left, hover black, active #222222 with shadow 0 and shift down-right.
2. Is it a read-only value? Then: JetBrains Mono 700-800 on #F9F9F9 with a 2px border and no shadow.
3. Is it a free-standing info object over the canvas? Then: white or #F9F9F9, 2px border, 3px shadow, no press behavior.
4. Is it a container that forms the layout? Then: white, 2px border, no shadow, 20px padding.
5. Does it report state? Then: one status color in one of the permitted forms, plus a glyph or word.
6. Does it interrupt? Then: dialog with 4px border and 8px shadow over the blurred scrim; never a toast for decisions.
7. Is it auxiliary, diagnostic or temporary? Then: dashed border.
8. Label it in uppercase Plus Jakarta Sans 800; put data in JetBrains Mono; put sentences in Inter.
9. If in doubt, make it bigger, blacker and flatter -- never softer.

---

## Bold Choices (Non-Negotiable)

1. Zero border radius: `border-radius: 0px !important` on the universal selector (sole exception: the status icon disc)
2. Geometric press shadow: hard-offset, zero-blur, pure black, on every pressable element
3. 3-state button mechanic: resting -> hover -> active with shadow collapse and translate, identical for every color variant
4. Touch-first sizing: primary-flow buttons 48px and taller; launcher buttons 200 x 70px
5. White canvas, black structure: no dark mode, no gray panels, no colored surfaces
6. Color means state: teal / amber / red / signal yellow only, each with a fixed meaning and a redundant glyph or label
7. One loud button per decision: at most one Signal, Confirm or Danger button per dialog
8. Uppercase everything that is a label: headings, buttons, form labels, badges
9. JetBrains Mono for all data: ports, versions, IDs, counts, percentages, logs
10. Square status LEDs with glyphs; moving diagonal stripes mean "working"
11. Concentric-square spinner: never a circular spinner
12. Instant view changes: dialogs, tabs and views switch without fade or slide
13. `@media (hover: hover)` guard on all hover effects
14. Kiosk layout: body never scrolls; launcher uses the 4-corner system; tools use work column + sidebar with the exit button pinned bottom-right
15. Full-screen help: the help system replaces the entire viewport
16. Inverted terminal: logs are white (or phosphor green) JetBrains Mono on pure black, collapsible and tappable to fullscreen
17. Dashed borders for auxiliary zones and key-value dividers only
18. Emoji + text: an icon never replaces a label

---

## Known Deviations in the Reference Tools (normalized here -- do not reproduce)

The reference code grew tool by tool. This file states the canonical value; these are the inconsistencies you would otherwise run into:

- Signal yellow #FFE600, button face #E5E5E5, pressed #222222 and all tints are hard-coded hex values there, not tokens. Some scripts reference undefined variables (`--error`, `--success`, `--shadow-inset-small`). Use the tokens defined above.
- Tints vary slightly per screen (#FFF9E6 / #FFF9DB / #FFFBEA for warning; #FFF5F5 / #FFE5E5 / #FFF0F0 for error; Google-style #E6F4EA / #E8F0FE / #FCE8E6 on device chips). Canonical: the four --tint-* tokens.
- Dialog cards exist in two builds: 4px border + 8px shadow, and an older compact card with 3px border and no shadow. Canonical: 4px + 8px.
- The scrim has `backdrop-filter: blur(4px)` or `blur(5px)` in the tools and none on the launcher. Canonical: 4px.
- Status LEDs: one tool has the glyph + stripe version (22px), two have a plain color square (24px) with an opacity pulse and a gray #E5E5E5 idle state. Canonical: glyph + stripes.
- Log terminal: white on black, green #00FF00 on black, and (in one tool) 10px black on white. Canonical: white on black, 13px.
- Log collapse timing is 0.2s ease in two tools and 0.3s cubic-bezier in two others. Canonical: 0.2s ease.
- Inputs: JetBrains Mono 700 / 15px / 48px in two tools, Inter 600 / 14px / 44px in two others. Canonical: mono, 48px.
- Checkboxes: custom 28px in one tool, native 20px with `accent-color: #000` in others. Canonical: custom 28px.
- Inline progress fill is black in one tool and teal in the others. Canonical: teal.
- Confirm buttons (teal) have white text where they are class-based and inherit black text where they are inline-styled. Canonical: white, weight 800.
- `.btn-sys` is 48px in two tools and 44px in two others. Canonical: 48px.
- A few inline controls are shorter than 32px (a 24px reprint button, padding-only correction buttons). Do not go below 32px.
- Tile hover is not wrapped in `@media (hover: hover)` there. Wrap it.
- `font-weight: 900` appears in inline styles although only 800 is loaded. Use 800.
- Inline `border-radius: 4px` appears on a few generated boxes and is neutralized by the global rule. It was never visible; omit it.
- The attention pulse on the settings container uses a blurred red glow. Canonical: border-color pulse only, no glow.
- Z-indices drift far apart between tools (dialogs at 1000 in one, 10000-30001 in another). Canonical: the layer table above.
- Update-overlay tokens differ marginally (#4A4A4A muted text, #D8D8D8 light border, #F7F7F7 muted surface). Canonical: the base palette.
- One page still links DM Sans. It is unused; the three families above are the complete set.
- Mode buttons with amber face appear more than once per screen in the help view (toggle + launch button). Canonical: one amber Attention button per screen; toggles may additionally be amber while active.

---

## Reproduction Checklist

Before calling a screen done, verify:

- [ ] Foundation stylesheet is loaded once; tokens are used instead of raw hex values
- [ ] No rounded corner anywhere; no blurred shadow; no gradient except the "working" stripes and the update backdrop dots
- [ ] Every button rests lifted with a hard shadow, inverts to black on hover (hover-capable devices only) and presses into its shadow
- [ ] Buttons in the primary flow are at least 48px tall; nothing interactive is below 32px
- [ ] Headings, buttons, labels and badges are uppercase Plus Jakarta Sans 800; all data is JetBrains Mono; sentences are Inter
- [ ] The page does not scroll; lists, logs and dialog bodies scroll internally
- [ ] Each dialog has a 4px border, 8px shadow, a clear heading, and at most one colored action button
- [ ] Every colored state has a glyph or label saying the same thing
- [ ] Color appears only on indicators, semantic buttons, accent bars, tints and status text -- never on layout structure
- [ ] Layout panels are flat; only pressable and free-floating objects carry shadows
- [ ] Dialogs, tabs and views switch instantly
- [ ] Exit / back is a red-text button pinned to the bottom of the sidebar
- [ ] It reads as an industrial control panel, not as a consumer app in black and white

---

## What Success Looks Like

A successfully implemented Neo-Industrial Monochrome interface should feel like:
- A professional piece of industrial control software -- reliable, no-nonsense
- An HMI panel that a warehouse technician would trust immediately
- A control terminal that works just as well in bright sunlight as in a dim server room
- A UI where every element`s purpose is immediately obvious without tooltips or onboarding

It should NOT feel like:
- A consumer app that happens to be black and white
- A generic Bootstrap or Material UI project
- Something that needs more color to feel complete
- Soft, friendly, or approachable -- it must feel authoritative
